  ('ee293e6b-5d9c-53b7-877a-41cf2940fd97', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g2-argument-evidence'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Listen. Cows give us lots of food. Milk comes from cows on dairy farms, and so do butter, cheese, and yogurt. Sheep help keep us warm. Their thick wool is cut each spring, and then it is spun into yarn. The yarn can be knit into cozy sweaters, hats, mittens, and socks. The author says wool is spun into yarn. Which point does that reason support? Tap the words.$q$, $q$[{"text":"Sheep keep us warm.","is_correct":true,"tile":{"kind":"word","value":"Sheep keep us warm."}},{"text":"Goats can climb.","is_correct":false,"tile":{"kind":"word","value":"Goats can climb."}},{"text":"Cows give us food.","is_correct":false,"tile":{"kind":"word","value":"Cows give us food."}}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Sheep keep us warm.$q$, $q$Wool spun into yarn for sweaters and socks is a reason that sheep help keep us warm. Milk, butter, and cheese are reasons that cows give us food.$q$, 'draft', null, 'audio_picture', null)
